    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Tikal, ancient Mutal, was one of the two great powers of the Classic Maya, a city of tens of thousands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peten">Petén</a> forest.</li><li>In 378 a warrior linked with Teotihuacan remade its dynasty, and its long rivalry with the Snake kingdom of Calakmul shaped Maya politics.</li><li>Victories in 695 and 743–744 restored its power in an age of giant temples, but its kings vanished in the ninth-century collapse.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Carved wooden lintels from Tikal's Temple IV, one of them celebrating a victory of 743, were taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> in 1877 and are still in a museum in Basel.</p></aside>
    </section>

